\section*{Factor-two shortest common superstring}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 128; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that ordinary shortest common superstring admits one deterministic polynomial-time factor-two algorithm on fully encoded string lists.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 128; 2026-09-24), a balanced graph of occurrences is organized into periodic layers. Maximal rotations and agreement constraints identify unblocked windows; collective incoming and outgoing connection rules link their closed walks while charging at most twice the optimal occurrence budget.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 128; 2026-09-24).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 128; 2026-09-24), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: ordinary shortest common superstring admits one deterministic polynomial-time factor-two algorithm on fully encoded string lists.

The selected statement includes the clause used for this problem.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{Superstring.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.Superstring.main}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

